WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.320
Most sensory receptors are excited by their stimulus.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.160
Rods and cones are turned down by theirs.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.800
How does quieting a cell let you see?

4
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.310
Start with a rod in the dark.

5
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.610
cGMP holds channels open in its outer segment.

6
00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.463
Sodium and calcium flow in.

7
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:26.683
This steady dark current keeps the rod depolarized, releasing glutamate all the

8
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:27.683
time.

9
00:00:28.407 --> 00:00:31.327
Potassium channels in the inner segment pull the other way.

10
00:00:32.187 --> 00:00:33.667
You have seen this tug of war before.

11
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:40.267
With the two pulls about equal, the voltage settles in between, near minus −40 mV.

12
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.490
Now a photon arrives.

13
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.470
It straightens the retinal inside rhodopsin, and the pigment becomes active.

14
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.480
One active rhodopsin switches on many transducins.

15
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.640
That is where much of the gain, the amplification, comes from.

16
00:00:56.583 --> 00:01:01.383
Transducin turns on an enzyme, PDE6, that destroys the messenger.

17
00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:04.383
The messenger falls, and the channels close.

18
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:09.507
With fewer cation channels open, potassium wins the tug of war.

19
00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:12.327
The balance point slides toward potassium.

20
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:16.747
The rod hyperpolarizes, and its glutamate release falls.

21
00:01:17.747 --> 00:01:19.187
Brighter light closes more channels.

22
00:01:20.087 --> 00:01:21.187
The response is graded.

23
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:27.390
Calcium falls too: fewer open channels let less calcium in, while the rod keeps

24
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.350
removing it. Low calcium speeds the remaking of the messenger.

25
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.390
The rod recovers, and turns its gain down in brighter light.

26
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:38.343
How small can the signal be?

27
00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:43.623
Baylor, Lamb and Yau drew the outer segment of one toad rod into a glass pipette.

28
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:47.123
They repeated a very dim flash many times.

29
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:49.263
Some flashes gave nothing.

30
00:01:50.203 --> 00:01:53.143
Many gave a dip of about the same size, near 1 pA.

31
00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:56.037
The sizes did not vary smoothly.

32
00:01:56.957 --> 00:02:00.657
Each event was one pigment molecule, activated by one photon.

33
00:02:01.637 --> 00:02:03.537
In dim light, a rod counts photons.

34
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:06.197
Two lights fall on one cone.

35
00:02:07.157 --> 00:02:09.197
One is dim, at the cone's best wavelength.

36
00:02:10.117 --> 00:02:11.617
One is brighter, far from it.

37
00:02:12.437 --> 00:02:13.617
Can the cone tell them apart?

38
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.570
Not if the two give it the same photon catch, with the same timing, under the same

39
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.850
adaptation. Once absorbed, every photon has the same effect, whatever its

40
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.850
wavelength.

41
00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:36.173
So one photoreceptor cannot separate a change in wavelength from a change in

42
00:02:36.173 --> 00:02:41.173
intensity. The takeaway: one receptor is univariant; the circuit compares.
